
  [1mSecond question — where does it live, and how long?[0m You said "persist," which rules out keeping it in a JavaScript
  variable, but "persist" spans three quite different lifetimes. This one is genuinely hard to change later: it
  determines what other forms can assume is present, and migrating stored data after the fact means handling users who
  have the old shape.

  - [1m[38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m, cleared on explicit logout.[0m Survives tab close and browser restart. Other forms can assume the ID is
    there on any visit. Readable by any JavaScript on the page — which matters a lot for a session token and much less
    for a non-credential identifier like this one. Matches "persists" most literally.
  - [1m[38;5;153msessionStorage[39m, cleared when the tab closes.[0m Same API, narrower blast radius; a shared or public machine doesn't
    leak the last user's ID to the next. But a returning user is unidentified until they log in again, and each tab is
    independent — two tabs are two separate worlds, which tends to surprise people.
  - [1mA cookie.[0m The only option the server sees automatically on every request, so it's the right answer if the [3mserver[0m
    needs the ID rather than your client code. But the secure form ([38;5;153mHttpOnly[39m) is unreadable from JavaScript, which
    directly contradicts "the caller passes it in" — you'd get server-side tracking and no client-side parameter. A
    non-[38;5;153mHttpOnly[39m cookie is readable but is strictly worse than [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m for this purpose.

  My recommendation is [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m. It's the only one that delivers all three things you asked for — passed by the
  caller, persistent, available to forms you haven't written yet — and its main drawback (script-readable) is acceptable
  precisely because we've scoped the ID to tracking rather than authorization. Moving to [38;5;153msessionStorage[39m later is a
  one-line change; moving to cookies is not.
